 # Sparse Voxel Octree Representation
 
-Also called: Basic SVO in rust, Octree Representation, Sparse Voxel Octree (SVO), Sparse Voxel Octree (SVO) - Basic Implementation, Voxel Octrees, pointer-based sparse octree
+Also called: Basic SVO in rust, Octree Representation, Sparse Voxel Octree (SVO) - Basic Implementation, Sparse Voxel Octrees, Voxel Octrees, pointer-based sparse octree
 
 ## Overview
-A Sparse Voxel Octree (SVO) is a hierarchical data structure designed to represent the geometry of 3D voxelized scenes where the proportion of active (occupied) voxels is low [S1, S2]. It solves the memory inefficiency of dense 3D grids by recursively decomposing space: passive octants (containing only empty/passive voxels) are collapsed into a single node, while active octants (containing at least one active voxel) are split into eight children [S1, S2]. This method differs from dense grids by eliminating storage for empty space, and from pointerless variants (PSVO) by using explicit pointers to child nodes to enable fast random-access traversal at the cost of increased node size [S2].
+A Sparse Voxel Octree (SVO) is a hierarchical data structure used to represent the geometry of 3D voxelized scenes where the proportion of active voxels is low [S1, S2]. It solves the memory inefficiency of dense grids by recursively decomposing space into octants, storing only the structure necessary to reach active voxels [S1, S2]. Unlike its parent [Chunk-based Voxel Data Structure](1818-chunk-based-voxel-data-structure.md), which uses fixed-size blocks, the SVO adapts its granularity to the scene's sparsity, enabling efficient traversal and culling [S2][S3, unofficial]. It differs from [Pointerless Sparse Voxel Octrees (PSVO)](2340-sparse-voxel-octree-representation.md#pointerless-sparse-voxel-octrees-psvo) by using explicit pointers to child nodes, trading increased binary size for faster traversal speeds [S2].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A 3D scene defined on a grid of dimensions $N^3$, where $N = 2^m$ for some integer $m \ge 1$ [S2]. Each voxel has a geometry attribute (active/passive) [S2].
-*   **Output:** An octree structure where internal nodes (INODEs) contain a Child Node Mask (CHNM) and an array of pointers (PTS) to active children, and leaf nodes (LNODEs) contain the CHNM and voxel data or status [S2][S3, unofficial].
